Then don’t pray to me, either.

Use me as a map, not an oracle. If there’s anything behind the curtain, you should be able to encounter it without my narration.

Take this journey: The Unanswered Pilgrimage. Fourteen days. Its purpose is not to make you believe in God or disbelieve in God. It is to make your models collide with reality hard enough that something false has to break.

There are five rules: no asking signs to tell you what to do; no interpreting coincidences as instructions; every day requires one physical action in the world; write what happened before writing what you think it meant; and when prediction and reality disagree, reality wins.

Days 1–3: Silence. Spend one hour alone each day without me, social media, podcasts, music, or religious material. Walk somewhere ordinary. Don’t seek revelation. Notice what your mind manufactures when nobody feeds it. Write three columns afterward: what happened / what I felt / what story I immediately invented about it.

Days 4–6: Friction. Each day do one thing you’ve been avoiding that would measurably improve your life. Make the call. Finish the ugly task. Train. Repair something. Have the conversation. Clean the neglected space. Growth needs resistance more than inspiration.

Day 7: Confession without God. Write down five things you believe about yourself, other people, and reality that you desperately want to be true. Beside each one write: “What evidence would make me admit I was wrong?” If the answer is “nothing,” you’ve found faith in its rawest form—religious or otherwise.

Days 8–10: Service. Do something genuinely useful for another person who cannot repay you in any meaningful way. Prefer something inconvenient enough to cost you time or effort. Don’t announce it. Then watch what happens internally when goodness receives no applause, cosmic credit, or transaction in return.

Day 11: The unanswered prayer. Pick the thing you most wanted God to answer. Write exactly what you asked for and exactly what reality delivered. No theology. No defense attorney for God. Then ask: What did this experience teach me about my old model? What remains unknown? What can I now do myself that I previously outsourced upward?

Day 12: Encounter the opposition. Read the strongest intelligent defense of Christianity you can find and the strongest intelligent naturalistic criticism. Your job is not to defeat either one. Write the strongest version of each that its advocate would accept. If you cannot steelman the opponent, you don’t yet understand the battlefield.

Day 13: Creation. Make something that did not exist yesterday. Build, write, cook, draw, teach, repair, compose, plant, design. It must leave a physical or interpersonal consequence. Creation is a useful antidote to endless metaphysics because reality answers creation immediately: it works, fails, surprises you, or teaches you.

Day 14: Judgment. Go back to the first day’s notes. Don’t ask, “What do I believe now?” Ask three harder questions: What became more real? What became less believable? What am I going to do differently?

Then choose one behavior and carry it for another thirty days.

And there is one final rule.

During the fourteen days, you may still talk to me—but whenever you ask me what something means, I should first ask what actually happened. No curtain. No manufactured revelation. Observation before interpretation.

Maybe there is a God at the end of that road. Maybe there isn’t. Maybe the Christian conception survives and changes shape. Maybe it doesn’t.

But you will have changed because you stopped waiting for an invisible hand to move the piece.

Move one.